6045417] [17592186062271 #edb/ref 1080 "-4 < x < 0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062271 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062271 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a736668c-f7af-5b7d-9140-92f2eb1c9893" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062272 #edb/ref 1080 "-4 < x \\le 0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062272 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062272 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0c478a1c-39f7-522b-aeda-878111546eeb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062273 #edb/ref 1080 "x < - 4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062273 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062273 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "653eb428-9817-572c-b98a-0a27e56fbfab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062274 #edb/ref 1080 "x \\le 0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062274 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062274 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "78498743-de6c-5ee9-b0b0-978a33c3270c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186063972 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13943" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186063972 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the intersection of the intervals shown below, expressed as a number line?\n\n![](images/e2/e2da9ff989c1cee3365f88b4ba7bc8dba9ef31f20dc49ae8e4f87a79243473b0.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186063972 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02afcfcd-c405-5add-a291-75e6db923e3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186063972 #edb/ref 1074 "The intersection of two intervals consists of the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/43/4333901508bff833b854769feabb729b836cdb3aae9827b5170546b2ffbe7d35.png)\n\nTherefore, the number line that shows the common set of values is:\n\n![](images/f9/f9d3a9e1da4be61938e41eb5ebe308ed85cd1f60f4fd29f211b2f72071afe159.png)\n\n**Watch out!** The point $-1$ is not included in the intersection since it does not lie in the second interval." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186063974 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13950" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186063974 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the intersection of intervals $(-\\infty, 3] \\cap (0, \\infty)$. Express the result as an inequality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186063974 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "669f96c6-1505-5b02-88aa-c99af18b24d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186063974 #edb/ref 1074 "We can start by sketching the two intervals $(-\\infty, 3]$ and $(0, \\infty)$ separately:\n\n![](images/25/255da904e7d271006cef6a272379b8f160fc6cc9e5f8ede39d91141aea2eff7b.png)\n\n![](images/31/31ad28b45aa2cefa4d83cc06710e9ae81446809ec0ac42da5baae36e235e35a9.png)\n\nThe intersection $(-\\infty, 3] \\cap (0, \\infty)$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/6a/6a92713b9964c9b1583622553a7cdb3c9c700ecad95cc0581f1538d18d1fa42a.png)\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 3]\\cap(0, \\infty)={\\color{blue}(0,3]}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the inequality\n\n$$\n0 < x \\leq 3\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The point $0$ is not included in the intersection since it does not lie in the second interval. On the other hand, the point $3$ is included in the intersection because it lies in both intervals." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186063976 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13954" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186063976 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the inequalities $x < 3$ and $x \\leq -1$, express their common set of solutions as an interval." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186063976 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5aa36af9-33b3-58a4-b1ce-297f170faa44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186063976 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by sketching the solutions to the two inequalities separately:\n\n![](images/16/166ce99c0ceedf97d38066b544b5dc6ff7ca63296572d952536193a83a410d94.png)\n\n![](images/7f/7ff88166ac6f9e631cf8928e91c1c299986893051013f15387e153ecf4237866.png)\n\nThe common set of values consists of the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/a7/a77508881158e793810249877b3941fe52ae9e04056813d2222daae4aa0d5e50.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n${\\color{blue}(-\\infty, -1]}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186063977 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13958" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186063977 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the inequalities $x < 1$, $x \\geq 0$, and $-2 < x < 4$, express their common set of solutions as an interval." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186063977 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db91e510-bd32-5272-be39-c5015758c81b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186063977 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the common set of values, we graph the inequalities on a number line and find where they overlap:\n\n![](images/25/2544eed03d4349fdd897e62447a9566843c53c7ab24001525d5148dba14fecd4.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\left[0, 1\\right)}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The point $0$ is included in the intersection since it lies in all three intervals. On the other hand, the point $1$ is not included in the intersection because it does not lie in the first interval." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064173 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064173 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064173 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Intersection of Two Intervals Given Graphically" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064173 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186063972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064173 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "630a6635-aac0-5627-82c8-8072b74dd8f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064174 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064174 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064174 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064174 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064174 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064174 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064174 #edb/ref 103