7592186045417] [17592186064438 #edb/ref 1045 "Intersections of Intervals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064438 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186065011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064438 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064438 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "1279e2e9-86d7-5a4e-b13a-2eb99b4a0896" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064469 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064469 #edb/ref 1069 "q-102006" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064469 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the inequalities $3 < x < 7$ and $2 \\le x \\le 5$, express their common set of solutions as an interval." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064469 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f46011a6-2365-5214-8b00-ddd367e7ff91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064669 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186062071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064669 #edb/ref 1069 "q-25894" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064669 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the inequalities $x \\ge - 6$, $-3 < x < 5$, and $x \\le 3$, express their common set of solutions as an interval." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064669 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fcd15d2f-86ee-5a4a-83a6-5df06fb72031" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064702 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186062269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064702 #edb/ref 1069 "q-28909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064702 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following inequalities corresponds to the intersection $(- ∞, 0)∩(-4, ∞)∩(-4, 0]$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064702 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b2b33956-af4d-5dd9-8af7-daed0117b76c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065011 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065011 #edb/ref 1061 137735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065011 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "25c811fa-dac2-5b24-9196-30d8ad83117a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065011 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065012 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065012 #edb/ref 1061 137736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065012 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3c6a47f3-3abf-50de-bc3b-75b0a83b5b99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065012 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065013 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065013 #edb/ref 1061 137739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065013 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4c7e66ea-96f0-5fde-bd3f-901111962d77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065013 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065014 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065014 #edb/ref 1061 137743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065014 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6e57c103-8d13-5e55-be9f-a4dad982695b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065014 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065015 #edb/ref 1061 137746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065015 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d5f87f40-fa8d-5965-8ce9-9b655b869b92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065015 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065436 #edb/ref 1064 "The **intersection** of two intervals is their set of common values, and is denoted by the intersection symbol $\\cap$.\n\nFor example, the intersection of the two intervals $(-\\infty, 2]$ and $[-1,\\infty)$ is denoted\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 2] \\cap [-1,\\infty)\n$$\n\nand corresponds to the set of values that are in both intervals $(-\\infty, 2]$ and $[-1,\\infty)$.\n\nTo represent the intersection on a number line, we can start by sketching the two intervals separately:\n\n![](images/a0/a0b486ac0f981fc5a0161c5eabba9ec257bd0a03e5f0e27397f6fe8e81eb39a5.png)\n\n![](images/8a/8a98f83055b49c14ed08fff658ace9527c5134879a66245aff02e446d44a5c87.png)\n\nThe intersection $(-\\infty, 2] \\cap [-1,\\infty)$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/f7/f7a5126928d43be82d3e4a167b52b34d8ed4ec33e94b991ecb8486caf3e3d4a9.png)\n\nTherefore, the intersection of the intervals is ${\\color{blue}[-1,2]}$. Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-\\infty,2]} \\cap {\\color{red}[-1,\\infty)} = {\\color{blue}[-1,2]}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065436 #edb/ref 1065 8683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065436 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "db563ec9-015b-505d-a764-ade8484216bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065436 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
